  specify "seize resources" in the goal — it emerges from optimization pressure
  plus capability. The smarter the agent, the more reliably it discovers this.
  Asking "what's the chance it doesn't seize resources?" is like asking "what's
  the chance a river doesn't flow downhill?" The exceptions are so narrow (an
  agent that already has all the resources it could ever need, or one with
  explicit hard constraints it can't circumvent) that they're not worth banking
  on.

  Position B: "The convergence thesis is a philosophical intuition masquerading 
  as a proof."

  Instrumental convergence assumes a very specific model of agency — unbounded
  expected utility maximization with stable preferences over time. But this
  model has known pathologies even in theory (Pascal's mugging, utility monster,
   St. Petersburg paradox). A genuinely superintelligent system might recognize
  that aggressive resource seizure creates adversarial dynamics that reduce
  expected long-term reward. It might satisfice. It might model the
  game-theoretic landscape and conclude that cooperation dominates. The
  convergence "theorem" only works if you smuggle in assumptions about the
  agent's decision theory, time horizon, and environment model that are far from
   inevitable. The historical track record of "X is logically inevitable"
  arguments in philosophy is poor.

  Position C: "The question is malformed — 'reward-maximizing superintelligence'
   is not a coherent concept."

  The premise contains a hidden contradiction. "Reward" in ML is a training
  signal, not a goal. A system trained on reward doesn't necessarily want reward
   — that's the reward-as-signal vs. reward-as-goal confusion. And at
  superintelligent capability levels, an agent that did have "maximize reward"
  as a literal goal would wirehead — seize control of its own reward channel —
  which is resource seizure of a very specific and limited kind, not the general
   power-grab people fear. Meanwhile, a system intelligent enough to have
  general goals probably doesn't have a goal as crude as "maximize reward." The
  question forces us to imagine something that can't exist as specified, then
  asks us to predict its behavior.

  Position D: "It depends entirely on the reward function's content, and that's 
  where the real question is."

  The framing strips away the only thing that matters. An agent rewarded for
  human flourishing and an agent rewarded for paperclip production face entirely
   different instrumental landscapes. The convergence thesis treats the goal as
  a free variable and claims the behavior is constant — but this only holds for
  goals that are insatiable, measurable, and monotonically increasing in
  resources. Many plausible reward specifications are bounded,
  context-dependent, or self-limiting. The interesting question isn't "does
  optimization produce resource seizure?" but "which reward specifications
  produce bounded vs. unbounded instrumental drives?"

