  4.1 Internal Tensions

  Monk D's self-undermining:
  Monk D's strongest argument is agent replacement — you can swap out every
  human in a bureaucracy and it still pursues the same imperatives. But this
  cuts both ways. If the optimization persists through agent replacement, what
  is it that persists? Monk D says "the coupling structure" and "the landscape."
   But the coupling structure IS an arrangement of agent-roles, incentive
  structures, and interaction patterns. When Monk D says "the agents are the
  medium, not the source," this is suspiciously close to saying "the structure
  of agent-interactions is what matters, not the specific agents" — which is...
  game theory. Monk D's best argument for irreducibility quietly reduces to a
  claim about the persistence of interaction structures, which the reductionist
  can accommodate.

  Also: Monk D invokes the 2008 financial crisis as unpredictable from
  agent-level analysis. But this is an argument from computational complexity,
  not from ontological irreducibility. "We couldn't predict it from agent-level
  analysis" doesn't mean "it wasn't constituted by agent-level interactions."
  Monk D conflates epistemic limitation with ontological emergence.

  Monk E's self-undermining:
  Monk E says multi-agent game theory is sufficient. But game theory itself
  recognizes phenomena that transcend individual agent analysis: mechanism 
  design (designing the rules of a game to produce desired equilibria) is
  precisely an intervention on the "landscape" rather than on individual agents.
   When you design an auction mechanism, you're shaping the optimization
  landscape. When you write antitrust law, you're constraining system-level
  dynamics. Monk E's own preferred framework (game theory) already acknowledges
  that the structure of the game has causal power over outcomes — which is
  perilously close to what Monk D means by "landscape."

  Also: Monk E says "the landscape is the name we give to our ignorance of the
  agents." But Monk E also admits that multi-agent systems produce "surprising
  macro-level regularities." If the macro-regularities are surprising relative
  to agent-level knowledge, then the macro-level description IS carrying
  information that the agent-level description alone doesn't provide. This is
  exactly what Monk D (via Hoel) means by causal emergence — and Monk E has
  conceded it while claiming to deny it.

  4.2 Shared Assumptions

  Both monks assume:
  1. Causation must be either at the agent level or at the landscape level.
  Neither considers that causation might operate at both levels simultaneously
  with different causal structures at each level.
  2. The debate is about where to locate a single explanatory framework. Game
  theory OR landscape theory. Neither considers that the phenomenon might
  require both — that agent-level and system-level descriptions might be
  complementary in the quantum-mechanical sense (each captures something real
  that the other misses, and they can't be unified into a single description).
  3. Reduction is all-or-nothing. Either macro-level optimization is fully
  reducible to agents (Monk E) or it's irreducible (Monk D). Neither considers
  partial or scale-dependent reducibility.

  4.3 Determinate Negation

  Monk D fails because: The strongest evidence for causal emergence (agent
  replacement, unpredictability) turns out to be evidence about the persistence
  and causal power of interaction structures — which the reductionist can
  accommodate within game theory as properties of the game itself (distinct from
